  "methodology": "Other",
  "methodology_other": "This product is derived by comparing current satellite-observed fAPAR values against\nthe long-term historical average for the same dekad or month. The resulting anomalies\nindicate whether the vegetation is performing better or worse than expected. It is\nfrequently used as the \"impact\" component in combined drought indicators, confirming\nthat meteorological deficits have translated into actual biophysical stress.",
  "name": "acmad-fapar-anomaly",
  "notes": "The fAPAR Anomaly dataset maps deviations in vegetation health and photosynthetic\nactivity across Africa. fAPAR measures the proportion of solar radiation absorbed\nby plants for photosynthesis, making it a robust biophysical indicator of vegetation\nstatus. The anomaly product highlights areas where vegetation activity is significantly\nbelow or above the long-term average, serving as a primary proxy for agricultural\nproduction and biomass availability.\n\n\nUnlike simple greenness indices (like NDVI), fAPAR is physically related to the\nenergy and mass exchange processes of the canopy. Negative anomalies in this dataset\nare strong indicators of vegetation stress caused by drought, pests, or disease,\nmaking it a critical input for yield forecasting and food security analysis.",
